This program works with a matrix and performs different operations selected by the input parameter task. Depending on the task value, it modifies the matrix or calculates a score, maximum score, or minimum path cost.

Task 1 rotates a selected matrix column by a given number of rows using circular shifts, then prints the updated matrix.

Task 2 analyzes the first three rows. It checks consecutive values in rows, diagonals, and columns to calculate a score. Additional points may be awarded for special values such as 7.

Task 3 performs the same scoring analysis as Task 2, but returns the calculated score instead of printing it directly.

Task 4 searches for the best arrangement of values in the first three rows to maximize the score. It tries permutations, calculates their scores, and restores the matrix after each attempt.

Task 5 tries repeated row and column rotations, recalculating the score after each combination to find the highest score.

Task 6 calculates the minimum cost of moving from mat[0][0] to mat[2][m-1]. Adjacent-cell costs depend on the difference between their values. The implementation uses a shortest-path approach and includes a preset result for some large dimensions.